CHAPTER V
RIGHTS OF VICTIMS
Article 13
Every victim has the right to obtain:
a. protection;
b. information;
c. optimal service;
d. continuous handling up to the rehabilitation stage;
e. confidential handling;
f. psychological and legal assistance; and
g. guarantee of the rights related to the victim's status as a family member as well as a member of society.

Article 14
In addition to the rights as referred to in Article 13, child victims of violence also receive special rights including:
a. the right to respect for survival, growth and development;
b. the right to basic services;
c. the right to equal protection;
d. the right to be free from various stigmas; and/or
e. the right to obtain freedom;
f. the right of victims and families to obtain facilitation in the judicial process.

3.2.text:
CHAPTER VII
INSTITUTIONS ORGANIZING PROTECTION FOR VICTIMS
Article 16
(1) The Regional Government establishes an Integrated Service Center (PPT) as the Organizing Institution for Prevention and Protection of Women and Children Victims of Violence in the region.
(2) PPT as referred to in paragraph (1) consists of elements:
a. health;
b. psychologist;
c. law enforcement;
d. social and empowerment;
e. education and religious affairs;
f. non-governmental organizations; and
g. private sector.
(3) Further provisions regarding PPT as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be regulated by Regent Regulation.

Article 18
(1) Prevention of acts of violence against women and children as referred to in Article 16 is carried out by the SKPD whose duties and functions are in the field of:
a. social affairs;
b. health;
c. education;
d. manpower;
e. women's empowerment and child protection;
f. mental health;
g. security and order;
(2) Prevention of acts of violence by the SKPD as referred to in paragraph (1) is carried out in an integrated and continuous manner based on existing regulations.

Article 3
Protection of Women and Children Victims of Violence aims to provide protection and services covering aspects of prevention, services and assistance, reunification, and empowerment.

Article 4 The implementation of protection for women and children from acts of violence aims to:
a. prevent all forms of acts of violence, including trafficking in persons;
b. eliminate all forms of violence and exploitation against women and children;
c. protect and provide a sense of security for victims of acts of violence;
d. provide services to victims of violence; and
e. carry out comprehensive recovery for victims.

CHAPTER VIII
PREVENTION OF ACTS OF VIOLENCE
Article 17
(1) The Government, in efforts to prevent the occurrence of acts of violence against women and children, shall carry out empowerment of families, parents, and communities by providing information, guidance and/or counseling.
(2) In addition to empowerment and awareness-raising as referred to in paragraph (1), regional governments shall undertake efforts as follows:
a. increase the quantity and quality of education, both formal and non-formal and informal;
b. open accessibility to obtain education, training, funding, income enhancement and social services;
c. endeavor to create employment opportunities for women;
d. build community participation and concern in the prevention of acts of violence against women and children;
e. build and provide a complete and easily accessible information system;
f. build networks and cooperation with law enforcers, government apparatus, universities and various non-governmental organizations that work and/or care for women and children victims of violence.

